    {
      "designator": "JCGM 100:2008 (GUM)",
      "topic": "Law of Propagation of Uncertainty (LPU), Type A/B contributors"
    },
    {
      "designator": "JCGM 101:2008 (GUM S1)",
      "topic": "Propagation of distributions using a Monte Carlo method and LPU validation"
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Linear Propagation (LPU) vs. Monte Carlo (MCM)",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Under the GUM Law of Propagation of Uncertainty (JCGM 100:2008), standard uncertainties combine by root-sum-of-squares weighted by first-order sensitivity coefficients: u_c = sqrt(sum((c_i * u_i)^2)), and a 95% expanded uncertainty assumes a Gaussian output distribution (k ≈ 1.96). That approximation is fast and exact for linear models dominated by Gaussian contributors.",
        "In dimensional metrology, two common situations break LPU: (1) a single rectangular (uniform) error source such as gauge resolution dominates the budget, producing flat-topped tails that Gaussian k = 1.96 over-covers; and (2) nonlinear measurands such as 2D radial true position r = sqrt(dx^2 + dy^2) near zero, where the first-order Jacobian vanishes and the output follows a skewed Rayleigh distribution. `fid-kunc` runs the JCGM 101:2008 clause 8 numerical tolerance check automatically so you see exactly when LPU agrees with Monte Carlo and when it fails."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Evidence Classes UEC-0 to UEC-3",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Not all uncertainty numbers carry the same evidence. Metrology Maven tags every ResultRecord with an Uncertainty Evidence Class: UEC-0 means uncertainty was not evaluated (and is never silently invented); UEC-1 marks declared or specification-derived budgets; UEC-2 requires characterized Type A / calibration-certificate sources; UEC-3 denotes ISO 15530-3 substitution or ISO/TS 15530-4 virtual-CMM evaluation."
      ]
    }
  ],
